  Summary

  Alpa (JAX/XLA + Ray):
    @parallelize(PipeshardParallel(stage_option="auto"))
    ├── AutoLayerOption: auto-group ops → layers
    ├── get_compute_cost(): profile every (stage,mesh,config) on real GPUs
    ├── training_dp(): DP finds best stage partition + submesh assignment
    ├── run_auto_sharding_pass(): XLA-embedded ILP for TP/DP per stage
    └── Full 3D (DP+TP+PP) in one call ✅

  ColossalAI (PyTorch + torch.distributed):
    autoparallelize()
    ├── ColoTracer: trace model → FX graph
    ├── StrategiesConstructor: enumerate sharding strategies
    ├── Solver (PuLP/CBC ILP): find best DP+TP plan
    ├── runtime_preparation/apply_pass: shard weights + insert collectives
    └── Only 2D (DP+TP) ✅, PP planning not yet integrated ❌

    colossalai/device/calc_pipeline_strategy.py: alpa_dp() ← ported, no caller yet
    colossalai/auto_parallel/pipeline_shard/__init__.py  ← empty, awaiting integration

  ColossalAI's advantage is that it's PyTorch-native (no JAX/XLA dependency, no Ray requirement), making it far more accessible for practitioners. Alpa is a research system — more complete in auto-planning but requires JAX and a Ray cluster,
  which is a significant operational barrier.